M|"E.T."
O|
O|
O|
M|Commentary by Richard Michaels
O|
O|
O|
M|TITLES: "E.T. - THE EXTRA-TERRESTIAL"
O|
M|[The letters are in soft-purple against a black background. Purple is
M|traditionally the color of that which is sacred.]
O|
S|EXT: NIGHT SKY: NIGHT
O|
N,T,G|The black screen becomes a night sky. The camera angles lowers to show a
N|forest against the night sky.
O|
S|EXT: LANDING SITE: NIGHT
O|
N|In an opening in the forest stands a spacecraft. The view of the craft is
N|obscured by tree branches. The atmosphere is misty, with blue lights coming
N|from the spacecraft.
O|
M|[The opening scene is misty and diffused. This forces the audience to pay
M|close attention to the images on the screen. The characters are not clearly
M|seen. This engages the audience, as they attempt to see what the aliens
M|really look like.]
O|
N|One creature walks up the gang blank and into the ship.
O|
T|INSERT: ALIEN HAND
O|
N|A strange hand, with two long and slender fingers protruding, move aside a
N|branch that obstructs the view.
O|
M|[This concentrates the audience's attention. The creature going into the
M|ship is being observed by another creature. Who are they? What's going on?
M|This is another technique that forces the audience to focus on the action.]
O|
S|INT: SPACECRAFT'S GREENHOUSE: NIGHT
O|
N|The inside of the ship appears to be a greenhouse. There are sounds of
N|water dripping. Cone shaped objects (possibly alien plants) sit among earth
N|plants. Vapors flow up from the plants.
O|
M|[These images all appear non-threatening. The aliens are inferred to be
M|collecting vegetation, and are thereby inferred to be harmless.]
O|
M|[Like many of Spielberg's other films, the opening sequences contain almost
M|no dialogue. The story is told without verbal exposition. He forces the
M|audience to become engaged in the storytelling process by giving them just
M|bits of information that they have to piece together into the story. He
M|doesn't insult their intelligence.]
O|
S|EXT: SPACECRAFT: NIGHT
O|
N|A group of the aliens work in front of the spacecraft. Suddenly, they hear
N|a dog howl, and they all stop working. Red lights begin to glow in their
N|chests. It appears as if their hearts have lit up at the sign of danger, as
N|the red glow seeps through their translucent skin. After a moment the red
N|lights dim and they return to their work.
O|
M|[This sets up the prop of the red-lights signifying danger. These small
M|creatures are endearing and non-threatening. They are like children, which
M|is immediate grounds for audience empathy. The thought that they may be in
M|danger from wild creatures in the forest, also creates empathy for them.]
O|
S|EXT: FOREST: NIGHT
O|
N|A small fern grows on the forest floor. An alien hand, with two fingers
N|protruding, reaches out for the fern. The alien groans. A rabbit turns and
N|listens. The fingers dig up the plant as the rabbit watches. The alien then
N|carefully uproots the plant. A small wayward alien walks alone among the
N|gigantic redwood trees. He's dwarfed by the huge trees.
O|
M|[The awesome towering trees psychologically creates audience empathy for
M|the creature. The audience identifies with him because they too feel small
M|when confronted by these trees.]
O|
S|EXT: HILLTOP: NIGHT
O|
N|The creature stands alone on a hilltop as he stares down at the city lights
N|below. Suddenly he lets out a moan of fright. A truck, with headlights
N|glaring, pulls up next to him.
O|
M|[The quiet, peaceful alien is now in jeopardy.]
O|
N|The creature runs from the lights. Several other trucks with head- lights
N|glaring drive up. Smoke flows from their exhaust pipes. Men's legs are seen
N|as they walk among the trucks. They step into a mud puddle as E.T. watches
N|from behind a shrub. A man with keys jangling from his waist walks past a
N|headlight. He carries a flashlight in his hands.
O|
M|[Keys have now become a prop which identifies the antagonist of the story:
M|the faceless government agent. Like the antagonist of many other fantasy
M|films, his face is not initially revealed in order to hold the audiences
M|attention.]
O|
N|The man with keys walks to a truck where he and two other men review a map
N|that's placed on the hood of the truck. The man with keys holds the
N|flashlight up and points it at the hood. E.T watches them from the bushes.
O|
M|[The fact that E.T. is observing the actions of the men also creates a
M|psychological bond between the audience and him, since they are also
M|observing these characters.]
O|
M|[While the audience doesn't actually see a map, they presume its existence
M|given the actions of the characters. This style of story telling engages
M|the audience, and gets them guessing about the characters' actions. They
M|then create expectations, which are later often proved to be wrong. This
M|makes the story both unpredictable and exciting.]
O|
S|EXT: SPACESHIP: NIGHT
O|
N|An alien stands in front of a round light and transmits a homing signal,
N|presumably to call the other aliens back to the ship.
O|
M|[These characters are all in jeopardy.]
O|
S|EXT: FOREST: NIGHT
O|
N|When E.T hears the sound, his red heart lights up. The homing signal
N|reverberates in his chest.
O|
M|[This establishes that his heart is used as a communication device, i.e.,
M|these aliens communicate with their hearts.]
O|
N|"Keys" hears this sound and quickly turns around. He points his flashlight
N|towards the sounds. The other men join him as they walk towards the sound.
N|E.T. screams and runs away, which is indicated by the shaking bushes. The
N|men with flashlights chase after him.
O|
M|[So, like Dorothy in the WIZARD OF OZ, the opening scene has the
M|protagonist being pursued by an unknown antagonist.]
O|
S|EXT: SPACECRAFT: NIGHT
O|
N|The lights dim on the footings of the spacecraft, presumably because the
N|spacecraft is preparing to takeoff.
O|
M|[The protagonist has the added jeopardy of being abandoned.]
O|
S|EXT: FOREST: NIGHT
O|
N|E.T. screams as he races for the spacecraft. The glow from his red-heart
N|reflects off the bushes as he runs towards the white light of the
N|spacecraft.
O|
M|[The audience still hasn't seen the face of the protagonist.]
O|
S|EXT: SPACECRAFT: NIGHT
O|
N|A lone alien stands before a large round white light, as he signals for the
N|other aliens to return to the ship.
O|
S|EXT: FOREST: NIGHT
O|
N|Men with flashlights run through the forest, as they pursue the red glowing
N|light racing towards the spacecraft. One of the pursuers is the man wearing
N|the keys on his waist.
O|
S|EXT: SPACECRAFT: NIGHT
O|
N|The grated gangplank lifts up, blocking the entrance to the ship. Behind
N|the grate stands the alien against the large round white light. The red
N|light of the aliens heart lights up as he stands behind the barrier.
O|
M|[This image exhibits an obstacle to E.T. returning to his ship.]
O|
S|EXT: FOREST: NIGHT
O|
N|The men reach the edge of the clearing and stop as they watch the
N|spacecraft lift off.
O|
N|One red lighted heart races along an old country fence towards the
N|departing spacecraft. Men with flashlights are still in pursuit. They stop
N|at a wooden gate as they watch the spacecraft fly away.
O|
N|E.T., with face obstructed by a branch, and with red-heart aglow, watches
N|the spacecraft fly away into the night. He utters a sorrowful moan.
O|
M|[He has been abandoned in a hostile alien world. This situation is
M|guaranteed to generate audience empathy.]
O|
N|The men with flashlights pointed up towards the sky watch the space- craft
N|fly away. They hear E.T.'s groan, and in unison point their flashlights in
N|his direction.
O|
M|[Not only is the small childlike creature abandoned, but he is still in
M|jeopardy of being captured by those who pursue him.]
O|
M|[This is the inciting event in E.T.'s story: his spacecraft has deserted
M|him.]
O|
S|EXT: HILLTOP: NIGHT
O|
N|Below lies the city, lit up against the dark night sky. E.T slowly makes
N|his way down the slope.
O|
N|Men with flashlights follow. They reach the hilltop, search the underbrush
N|for the alien, then start down the slope after him.
O|
M|[This concludes the prelude to the story. Both the protagonist and
M|antagonist have been introduced, and audience empathy has been established
M|for the protagonist. The protagonist's primary objective is to survive and
M|find a way home, while the antagonist's primary objective is to capture the
M|alien.]
O|
S|EXT: SUBURBAN HOUSE: NIGHT
O|
N,G|[This is an establishing shot.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: NIGHT
O|
N|A group of boys are sitting around a table playing a DUNGEONS AND DRAGONS
N|game. One boy is on the telephone ordering a pizza. They throw wads of
N|paper at each other as they play.
O|
N,I|Elliott, the youngest boy, sits behind the counter. He's separated from the
N|other boys. After a few moments he yells that he's ready to play the game.
O|
M|[The character's positions are structured to emphasize Elliott's separation
M|from the others.]
O|
N|One of the boys tells Elliott that he can't join when they are in the
N|middle of the game.
O|
M|[Audience empathy is generated for the young child who is being excluded
M|from the game.]
O|
N,I|Elliott walks up to the table and yells at his brother, Mike. Michael says
N,I|that Elliott has to ask Steve, who is the Game Master, and who has absolute
N|power.
O|
M|[The notion of "absolute power" is set up here, for a "payoff" later when
M|Elliott uses it to keep Michael silent about E.T.]
O|
M|[Elliott's objective here is to play the game, but also to be accepted by
M|the group and gain the respect of the older boys.]
O|
N|Steve tells Elliott that first he has to wait for the pizza, then when he
N|brings it back he can play. Steve gives him money for the pizza. Elliott
N|picks up his baseball and glove, then leaves the room.
O|
M|[The ball and glove are props that will be used in the story.]
O|
M|[Elliott's sub-objective is to get the pizza. Once this is accomplished,
M|he'll be able to play the game.]
O|
S|EXT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: NIGHT
O|
N|It's a rainy, misty night. Elliott, with ball and glove in hand wait at the
N|bottom of the driveway for the pizza. A car pulls up and parks in the
N|driveway. Elliott pays the driver, then takes the pizza box from him as the
N|car pulls out of the driveway.
O|
N|Elliott walks up the driveway and into the garage. He hears a noise in the
N,U|backyard, then calls out the name "HARVEY," presumably his dogs name.
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: NIGHT
O|
N|Elliott's mother wears an orange outfit as she works in the kitchen. She
N|bends over to put dishes into the dishwasher. When she does this one of the
N|young boys reaches out to put his finger on her rear. Mike yells for him to
N|stop. He pulls his finger away.
O|
S|INT: PATIO: NIGHT
O|
N|Elliot walks past the ping pong table when he hears more noises in the
N,U|backyard. Again he calls out the name "Harvey."
O|
M|[This generates tension and suspense in the audience.]
O|
N|With the pizza box in hand, Elliot walks past the patio table and towards
N|the shed in the backyard. A bright crescent moon hangs in the night sky.
O|
M|[This adds enchantment to the scene.]
O|
N|There is a mist in the air, and a bright light shines in the shed. Elliott
N|places the pizza box on the ground as he approaches the shed. He's still
N|carrying the baseball and glove as he stands in front of the shed's
N|entrance. He tosses the ball into the shed. After a few seconds the ball is
N|tossed back out at him and hits him on the foot.
O|
M|[This is the payoff to the setup of the baseball prop. This event surprises
M|both Elliott and the audience.]
O|
N|Elliott becomes frightened, turns and steps on the pizza box as he runs
N|towards the house.
O|
M|[This is the inciting event in Elliott's story: there is some creature
M|hiding out in his shed that will change his whole life.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: NIGHT
O|
N|Elliott's mother is talking to the boys when Elliott runs into the house
N|screaming that there's something in the tool shed. He tells them that it
N|threw a ball at him, but they continue to ignore him until he screams
N,U,C|"QUIET!" "Nobody go out there," says Elliott. The boys immediately jump up
N|from the table. Two boys grab kitchen knives as Michael tells his mother to
N|stay inside. He and his friends will check it out. She yells at them to put
N|the knives back as she follows them outside.
O|
S|EXT: SHED: NIGHT
O|
N|The light is still on in the shed.
O|
S|EXT: PATIO: NIGHT
O|
N|Elliott's mother walks into the yard with a flashlight in her hand. She is
N|surrounded by the boys as they approach the shed. The mother says that
N|there's nothing in there as Michael walks through the entrance. Michael
N,U|bends down and finds tracks on the dirt floor. "The coyote's come back
N,U|again, Ma." he says. His mother becomes frightened and orders them all back
N|into the house.
O|
M|[Jeopardy is introduced into the situation. Even though the audience knows
M|that it is probably the alien in the shed, the scene increases the
M|tension.]
O|
N|On his way back to the house, Michael sees the pizza box. He bends down,
N|opens the box, then takes out a piece. He's angry at Elliott. Elliott tries
N|to explain that it was just an accident, but his brother is still angry.
N|When the mother asks who ordered the pizza, Elliott points to one of Mike's
N|friends. This makes the friend mad at Elliott. Elliot tries to persuade his
N|mother that there is a creature in the shed, but she doesn't believe him.
N|They walk into the house.
O|
M|[Empathy is generated for Elliott because the two boys are unfairly angry
M|at him and his mother doesn't believe him.]
O|
S|INT: SHED: NIGHT
O|
N|The alien's fingers point out into the shed entrance, then wrap around the
N|side of the door. The sound of his heavy breathing is heard.
O|
M|[This establishes for the audience that he really is in the shed. They feel
M|sorry for Elliott because he was telling the truth and no one believed
M|him.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S BEDROOM: NIGHT
O|
N|The clock on the end table indicates 2:00. Next to the clock is a picture
N|of a dog, presumably Harvey. This same dog lies asleep in the bed. He hears
N|a noise and picks up his head. He is in the lower half of a bunk bed. On
N|the top bunk sits Elliott. He is awake as he listens to the noise outside.
O|
M|[Elliott's objective is to prove there's a creature in the shed.]
O|
S|EXT: BACKYARD: NIGHT
O|
N|Elliott opens a gate and walks into the backyard. He carries a flashlight
N|in his hand and shines it into the cornfield next to his house. He walks
N|into the field.
O|
S|EXT: CORNFIELD: NIGHT
O|
N|Elliott walks into the cornfield. He whistles, and gets entangled in the
N|cornstalks as he walks. He sees tracks in the dirt with his baseball
N|nearby. He continues to walk through the field.
O|
M|[The stalks are obstacles that obstruct both his path and his vision. It
M|also clutters the visuals and forces the audience to pay closer attention.
M|The suspense increases because they aren't sure whether he'll meet up with
M|the alien or the coyote.]
O|
N|Elliott pushes aside some stalks and shines the light into the face of E.T.
N|Together, in unison, the scream. Elliot drops his light, then continues to
N|scream as he looks around the field. E.T. cries out and runs away through
N|the cornfield.
O|
M|[Their reactions upon seeing each other were identical. This is the first
M|instance of a series of situations which will "merge" their characters to
M|such an extent that whatever empathy the audience has for one they will
M|also have for the other.]
O|
S|EXT: BACKYARD: NIGHT
O|
N|Elliott runs into his backyard. His play swings sway back and forth in the
N|air, as if someone had just bumped into them. Two garbage pails fall over
N|near an open metal gate at the top of a stone stairway. The garbage rolls
N|out of the cans and down the stone stairs. A red light by the gate lights
N|the stone stairway.
O|
M|[The audience psychologically associates this red light with E.T.'s red
M|lighted heart, and makes the inference that he had run up the staircase and
M|out the gate.]
O|
N|Elliott has an expression of wonderment and gratification on his face as he
N|looks up the staircase, for he has just established the existence of the
N|creature in the shed.
O|
S|EXT: GARAGE: DAY
O|
N|Elliott rides down the driveway on his bicycle. He next rides down a dirt
N|road that overlooks the suburban community.
O|
M|[Elliott's new objective is to locate the creature.]
O|
S|EXT: FOREST ROAD: DAY
O|
N|Elliott holds a bag of candy in one hand as he pours some into the other
N|hand. He tosses candy onto the round as he walks through the woods calling
N|out for the creature.
O|
N|In another part of the woods Elliott lays his bike down, then continues to
N|toss candy and yell out "hello!" He sees a man on the road below and
N|quickly hides behind a tree.
O|
M|["Keys," the faceless antagonist, is still pursuing the alien.]
O|
N|Elliott runs back to his bike, then rides down the forest road past a wire
N|fence. The alien's fingers side down the side to the tree. He was watching
N|Elliott.
O|
M|[Elliott is in jeopardy both from the faceless man and the faceless alien.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: DAY
O|
N|Elliott's mother sits at the tale with the dog, Harvey, at her side.
N|Elliott, Michael and Gertie are eating supper.
O|
M|[This is a home without a father, which creates more empathy for the
M|protagonist.]
O|
N|Gertie asks Elliott what he's going as for Halloween. He's depressed, and
N|replies that he's not going. Mike mocks him by suggesting that he go as a
N|goblin. Elliott snaps back, telling Mike to shut up. The mother tries to
N|explain that it's not that they don't believe him, but Elliott cries out
N|that the creature was real.
O|
M|[The audience also knows that the creature is real. This generates more
M|empathy for the protagonist, because he is mocked and not believed when he
M|is expressing the truth.]
O|
N|Mike offers other suggestions: perhaps it was an iguana, a deformed child,
N,U,C|or maybe an Elf or a Leprechaun. Elliott gets angry and shouts "It was
N,U,I|nothing like that, penis breath." Mary, his mother shouts at him to sit
N,U,C|down. Elliott responds with "Dad would believe me." Mary replies "Maybe you
N,U|ought to call your father and tell him about it." "I can't. He's in Mexico
N,U,C|with Sally" says Elliott. Both Mary and Mike are upset, while Gertie asks
N,U|"Where's Mexico?" Mary excuses herself and leaves the table. She walks to
N,U|the window as Mike tells Elliott that he's going to kill him. "If you ever
N,U|see it again, don't touch it. Just call me and we'll have someone come and
N,U,C|take it away," says Mary. "Like the dog catcher?" says Gertie. "But they'll
N,U,C|give it a lobotomy, or do experiments on it or something," says Elliott.
O|
M|[This sets up the audience's expectations about the harm that will befall
M|the alien when he is captured.]
O|
N|After a bit of dialogue concerning who should be doing the dishes, Mary
N,U|leaves the room saying "He hates Mexico."
O|
M|[This scene explores the emotional relationships between the family members
M|in greater detail. Empathy is also generated for Mary who was abandoned by
M|her husband. She is left with the responsibility of raising the children
M|while he's traveling with his new girlfriend to Mexico, a country which he
M|hates.]
O|
N|Elliott takes some dishes to the sink, turns on the faucet and gazes out
N|the kitchen window as water steams up from the sink.
O|
S|EXT: BACKYARD: NIGHT
O|
N|A crescent moon floats in the sky. A bright light is on in the shed. In
N|front of the shed Elliott lies inside a sleeping bag on a folding chair
N|with a flashlight in his hand. He hears the sound of feet walking in the
N|underbrush.
O|
N|Elliott sits up to see the alien standing in front of the shed. The alien
N|groans as Elliott freezes from fear in his chair. He tries to call out for
N|his mother, but can't get the words out of his mouth. He next tries to call
N|Mike, but fear makes him choke on his words.
O|
N|The alien approaches Elliott. The bright light from the shed makes it
N|impossible to see his face: only the outline of his form is visible. The
N|alien walks right up to the edge of the lawn chair.
O|
M|[The scene is filled with suspense because Elliott may be in jeopardy from
M|the creature.]
O|
N|The alien slowly moves its hand over the chair, then drops candies onto the
N|blanket. Elliott relaxes.
O|
M|[This non-verbal gesture by E.T. demonstrates that he is harmless. The
M|tension in the scene is thereby released. Elliott has achieved his
M|objective: he established contact with the alien.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: NIGHT
O|
N|Elliott places candy on the carpet, then walks backwards down the hallway
N|towards his room. E.T. walks up the stairs and reaches out for the candy,
N|which he then takes into his hand and eats.
O|
M|[Elliott's new objective is to get the alien into his room. His main
M|obstacles are to somehow persuade the alien to come into the house, while
M|not waking his family.]
O|
M|[This scene also is a good example of how to structure a scene's beginning.
M|In real life, the scene would have started in the backyard, tracked through
M|the kitchen, the living room and then up the stairs. But since all of that
M|information would have been redundant, picking the action up from the top
M|of the stairs was sufficient.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM: NIGHT
O|
N|Elliott places candy on the floor by the entrance to his room. A sign on
N|his door contains the word "ENTER." E.T picks up the candy.
O|
N|Elliott has run out of candy. He goes to his desk in search for more. He
N|opens some drawers, but doesn't find any. When he returns to the doorway,
N|E.T. has already entered the room. E.T. pulls a box down from the table,
N|then knocks a can filled with pens onto the floor. This makes a loud noise,
N|and forces Elliott to quickly close the door to his room.
O|
M|[The audience still does not have a clear view of E.T.'s face nor body.
M|This holds their attention during the scene. They are waiting for that
M|wonderful moment.]
O|
N|Elliott throws a blanket over E.T. as they stare at each other. E.T. is
N|finally revealed to the audience.
O|
N|Elliott moves his hand in front of his face, a gesture indicating
N|bewilderment. E.T. mimics this action.
O|
M|[This is the beginning of their efforts to communicate, but also the start
M|of the audience emotionally identifying the two characters as one object of
M|empathy.]
O|
N|Elliott places his fingers to his lips to see if E.T. will respond in the
N|same manner, which he does. Elliott then places one finger to his ear,
N|which E.T. also mimics. Elliott smiles, then holds his left hand up with
N|all five fingers spread apart. E.T. raises his left hand with three fingers
N|spread apart. Elliott closes his fingers until there is only one remaining
N|up. E.T. does the same. They both wiggle this one finger. A poster on the
N|wall between Elliott and E.T. has the word "SUPERSTAR" printed on it.
O|
M|[These actions help the audience to conceive of these two characters as
M|"mirror images" of one another, worthy of equal empathy. It also bonds the
M|young children in the audience to them, because the characters are speaking
M|in a language they too can understand. All the children in the audience
M|have just been through the experience of learning a language. They can
M|easily identify with the problems that that two characters in the story are
M|having in communicating with each other. This forges a bond between the
M|children and both Elliott and E.T. This also forms the basis for the repeat
M|viewing of this film which was essential for its phenomenal box-office
M|success.]
O|
N|Suddenly, Elliott becomes tired and yawns, then sways as if in a trance. He
N|slowly walks backwards and sits down in a chair. Elliott seems asleep as
N,G|the camera moves up for a closeup of Elliott's face.
O|
M|[Presumably, E.T. has used his telepathic powers to place Elliott into a
M|trance in order to merge their minds and feelings.]
O|
M|[This is the end of the first act. Elliott has succeeded in gaining
M|possession of the dramatic objective E.T.), and has him in his room. But
M|E.T. has also taken possession of Elliott by merging their feelings, so
M|that they both become one protagonist. The second act will end when they
M|are both captured by the antagonist.]
O|
S|EXT: FOREST: DAY
O|
N|Mist envelops the fence and gate in the section of the forest where E.T
N|originally landed. Men with flashlights and floodlights search throughout
N|the area. "Keys" walks into the forefront, holding a flashlight in his
N|hand. He bends to the ground and finds some candy.
O|
M|[The objective of the faceless antagonist is to capture the alien. The
M|candy is a prop that will lead them to Elliott's house just as Elliott used
M|them to lead E.T. into his room. This scene shows that the protagonist is
M|in jeopardy, because he is still being pursued.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM: DAY
O|
N|Elliott lies in bed with a thermometer in his mouth. His head rests on an
N|orange pillow (same color as his mother's dress in the first scene) while
N|his mother sits besides him. She takes out the thermometer, reads it, then
N,U|says "Okay, you're hot." She then leaves the room. Elliott quickly swings a
N|lamp back over his head and heats the thermometer. He also places a blue
N|heating pad over his face, to heat himself. He then places the thermometer
N|back into his mouth, swings the lamp away, and places the heating pad under
N|the covers as his mother enters the room. She opens his closet and
N|separates the hanging clothes as she walks into the closet. Elliott sits up
N|in bed and intensely watches her.
O|
M|[Elliott's objective is to stay home from school in order to be with E.T.
M|His obstacle is his mother, who wants him to go to school. To overcome this
M|obstacle he must persuade her that he's sick. A crisis occurs when she
M|enters the closet in which E.T. is hiding. This creates jeopardy for the
M|protagonist.]
O|
S|EXT: DRIVEWAY: DAY
O|
N|Mike backs the car down the driveway.
O|
T|INTERCUT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM
O|
N|Mary walks out of the closet carrying a blue sleeping bag. Elliott is
N|tense, then relieved, once he realizes that she didn't find E.T. He falls
N|back into bed.
O|
T|INTERCUT: DRIVEWAY
O|
N|Mike backs the car over part of the lawn.
O|
M|[This scene functions not only as comic relief, but also as a set up for
M|the scene here Mike has to drive the van during E.T.'s escape sequence.
M|This demonstrates both his incompetence, and that he is only allowed to
M|drive the car backwards down the driveway.]
O|
T|INTERCUT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM
O|
N|Mary covers Elliott with the blue sleeping bag as Elliott puts the
N|thermometer back into his mouth (first backwards, then he corrects it).
O|
M|[Humor is generated because the audience knows that he is fooling his
M|mother.]
O|
T|INTERCUT: DRIVEWAY
O|
N|Mike continues to back the car down the driveway. He overshoots the edge
N|and goes out into the street. He then pulls forward across the sidewalk.
O|
T|INTERCUT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM
O|
N,U,C|"You were outside waiting for that thing?" says Mary. Elliott nods.
O|
M|[Elliott is covered in blue-white colors, which traditionally signify
M|innocence, and which generates audience empathy for him.]
O|
N|She tells him not to watch TV, kisses him, then leaves the room. Elliott
N|quickly closes the blinds.
O|
S|EXT: DRIVEWAY: DAY
O|
N|Mary runs down the driveway towards the car. Gertie points out the patches
N|of lawn that Mike pulled onto the driveway as he was driving the car. Mary
N|pulls her into the car.
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM: DAY
O|
N|Elliott gestures for E.T. to walk out of the closet, which he does. He is
N|wearing Elliott's robe. Elliott asks him if he talks.
O|
M|[Elliott's objective is to communicate with E.T., his obstacle is that E.T.
M|does not speak the English language.]
O|
N,U,C|"Me Human. Boy. Elliott, Elliott, Elliott" says Elliott.
O|
N|E.T. turns away and walks towards the work table. Elliott picks up objects,
N|such as a can of coke and toy soldiers, from the table then names them for
N|E.T. Elliott points to the fish and explains how the fish eat the fish
N|food. He places a wooden toy shark into the fishbowl, then states that the
N|shark eats the fish but nobody eats the shark.
O|
M|[In his effort to communicate with E.T., Elliott tries to teach him the
M|language by showing him objects that are used in everyday life. This
M|process is similar to that experienced by the children and parents in the
M|audience everyday as they also learn and attempt to communicate with each
M|other. This type of scene solidifies the audience's emotional bonding with
M|Elliott and E.T.]
O|
N|E.T. takes a toy car into his mouth on chews on it.
O|
M|[Like many a child would.]
O|
N|Elliott takes this to mean that E.T. is hungry. Elliott tells E.T. to stay
N|where he is.
O|
M|[Elliott's new objective is to get food for E.T. and himself.]
O|
N|Elliott opens the door and Harvey tries to run into the room. E.T. groans
N|with fear and backs into a corner as Elliott pulls Harvey out of the room.
O|
M|[This introduces jeopardy into the scene, for Harvey could harm E.T.]
O|
S|INT: KITCHEN: DAY
O|
N|Elliott, dressed only in white underwear, opens the refrigerator door and
N|takes out some food.
O|
T|INTERCUT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM
O|
N|E.T. walks around the room.
O|
T|INTERCUT: KITCHEN
O|
N|Elliott opens up a jar of peanut butter.
O|
T|INTERCUT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM
O|
N|E.T. touches a tennis racket.
O|
T|INTERCUT: KITCHEN
O|
N|Elliott picks up cheese and tomatoes.
O|
T|INTERCUT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM
O|
N|E.T. picks up an umbrella.
O|
T|INTERCUT: KITCHEN
O|
N|Elliott takes a carton of milk out of the refrigerator.
O|
T|INTERCUT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM
O|
N|E.T. accidentally opens up the umbrella.
O|
T|INTERCUT: KITCHEN
O|
N|Elliott screams with fright and drops the milk carton onto the floor.
O|
T|INTERCUT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM
O|
N|E.T. screams with fright, drops the umbrella, then runs into the closet.
O|
M|[This further establishes the merging of E.T. and Elliott into one being.
M|Elliott shares E.T.'s feelings and simultaneously experiences what he
M|experiences. The audience also experiences this surprise with them, which
M|further tightens the bonds.]
O|
N|Milk flows from the open carton onto the floor, as Elliott leans into the
N|open refrigerator. He clutches his chest and looks up towards the ceiling
N|as he ponders his experience.
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM: DAY
O|
N|Elliott carries a plate of food into the room. He walks past the open
N|umbrella and looks into the closet. Stuffed animals line the back of the
N|closet wall. E.T. pushes the animals forward. He was hiding behind them. He
N,U|is still shaking from the fright experience. "Are you okay? Too much
N,U|excitement, huh? You want a coke?"
O|
M|[This is the resolution of the last scene sequence. We see both characters'
M|reactions to the events that have occurred.]
O|
S|EXT: DRIVEWAY: DAY
O|
N|Michael runs up the driveway towards his house. He's wearing a red football
N|blazer.
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: DAY
O|
N|Michael opens the front door and runs into the house. He takes off his red
N|shirt to reveal the blue shirt he's wearing underneath. He picks up a
N,U|magazine then opens the refrigerator. "Nothing but health shit," he says,
N|as he takes out a can and shakes it.
O|
M|[The audience tenses up because jeopardy has just been introduced into the
M|story. They wonder what will happen when Mike discovers E.T. in Elliott's
M|bedroom.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM: DAY
O|
N|Elliott walks away from his closet and opens the door to his room. He tells
N|Mike to come in. Both boys are dressed in blue and white colors. Mike calls
N|Elliott a faker, then starts to tell him about scores on an asteroid game,
N|when Elliott interrupts, to tell him about the goblin. Michael makes fun of
N|Elliott and his belief that the goblin has returned. But Elliott persists,
N|and makes Mike promise to give Elliott "absolute power."
O|
M|[This is the payoff to the "absolute power" concept as set up during the
M|DUNGEON AND DRAGONS scene.]
O|
M|[Elliott's objective is to vindicate himself to his brother and sister by
M|proving that "the Goblin-E.T." really does exist.]
O|
N|Ellott makes Mike close his eyes, then he retrieves E.T. from the closet
N|and leads him to the center of the room. Mike has a silly grin on his face
N|as he opens his eyes and turns around to look at E.T. A sickening look of
N|terror comes over his face.
O|
N|Suddenly Gertie opens the door and walks into the room with her doll under
N,U|her arm, saying "Elliott, look what I made for you." E.T. sees her then
N|stretches his neck to raise his head high. Gertie walks right up to him and
N|screams. E.T. screams back at her. Elliott screams for both of them to
N|stop. Mike falls back against the wall and knocks down the bookcase. They
N|all scream together. Elliott grabs Gertie's mouth and pushes her back to
N|Mike.
O|
N,U,P,C|"Kids, I'm home," shouts Mary from downstairs.
O|
M|[Jeopardy! The mother, an adult, is about to discover E.T. Even though they
M|are terrified, the children all band together against the threat. They'd
M|rather sit in the closet with the "monster" than have their mother discover
M|him.]
O|
N|Mike quickly shuts the door, then they all run to the closet. Gertie
N|screams again. Mike grabs her by the mouth and pulls her away as E.T. runs
N|across the room with his arms raised. Elliott shuts the closet door just as
N|his mother walks into his room. Mary notices the fallen bookcase. She looks
N,U|around and then comments, "This is no room, this is an accident." Elliot
N|tries to cover by saying he was "reorganizing."
O|
S|INT: CLOSET: DAY
O|
N|E.T.'s head appears among the stuffed animals. Mike has his hand over
N|Gertie's mouth as she tries to scream. Mary walks past the closet door.
O|
M|[This creates suspense in the audience: will E.T. be discovered by Mary?]
O|
N|Mary asks Elliot to keep an eye on Gertie while she takes a shower. She
N|kisses him on the head then leaves the room. Elliott opens the closet door.
N|Michael, in awe with mouth open wide, watches as Elliott approaches E.T.
O|
N,U,P,C|"Elliott," whispers Mike. "I'm keeping him," says Elliott.
O|
N|E.T. sits behind the stuffed animals as if he was one of them.
O|
M|[The use of stuffed animals as props around E.T helps to transfer positive
M|feelings that the audience has for them to E.T.]
O|
N|Gertie asks what it is as she and Michael approach E.T. Elliott tells her
N|that it won't hurt her. When asked if it's a boy or girl, Elliott replies
N|that it's a boy. He then tells her that she can't tell Mom about the
N,U|creature. When asked why not, he explains that "grownups can't see him.
N,U,C|Only little kids can see him." Gertie snaps back, "Give me a break."
O|
M|[The charm in Gertie's character lies in the contradictory attributes of
M|being a sophisticated child. Humor is generated by this incongruity.]
O|
N,C,P,U|Elliott then goes into his Dracula voice: "Then do you know what will
N,U|happen if you do tell?" He grabs Gertie's doll, tosses it to Mike and tells
N,U|him to "Do it!." Mike "tortures" the doll by twisting its arm. Gertie cries
N|as she runs to it. E.T. watches the scene with wonderment in his eyes.
N|Gertie finally promises not to tell about E.T. They all watch him as he
N|stretches his neck and lifts up his head.
O|
S|EXT: COMMUNITY: DAY
O|
N|On the hilltop overlooking the neighborhood stands a man with a camera as
N|he takes photos of the houses below. The man with keys on his waist walks
N|into the area, as other men comb the ground with electronic equipment.
O|
M|[Jeopardy is introduced in the scene in order to maintain tension in the
M|audience. The antagonist is getting closer to the home of the protagonist.]
O|
S|EXT: ELLIOTT`S HOUSE: NIGHT
O|
N,G|[This is an establishing shot.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT`S HOUSE: NIGHT
O|
N|Mary, carrying a yellow watering pail, walks across the room and waters a
N|large plant. Gertie pulls a little red wagon into the hallway. When asked
N|by Mary what she's doing, Gertie replies that she's going to play in
N,U,C|Elliott's room. "Okay, don't let them torture you," says the mother. "I
N,U,C|won't, Mary," replies Gertie.
O|
M|[This generates humor because the child deals with the mother as if they
M|are equals.]
O|
N|Gertie picks up a pot containing a sickly geranium plant and places it into
N|the wagon. The phone rings and Mary answers it off screen.
O|
M|[The plant becomes an important prop because in the story it will represent
M|the health and well-being of E.T.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM: NIGHT
O|
N|Gertie knocks on the door. Mike lets her and Harvey into the room. They
N|walk to the table where E.T. and Elliott are sitting. Gertie puts the
N|sickly plant n the table. E.T. puts his finger into the plant. Elliott
N|opens up an encyclopedia and points to California on a map of the United
N,U,C|States. "We are here," Elliott says as E.T. munches on celery. Michael
N|hands Elliott a globe and tells him to use this instead of the map. Elliott
N|points to the globe, then asks E.T. where he's from. E.T. points out the
N|window.
O|
M|[Elliott's objective is to discover more about the alien.]
O|
N|Elliott opens the book to a page that has the map of a galaxy. He points to
N,U|the planet earth, touches the globe, then says "HOME, HOME." E.T. makes a
N|soft noise that sounds similar to the word "home." Gertie watches him as he
N|picks up round objects and places them on the map: three small pieces of
N|fruit and two eggs. E.T.'s eyes concentrate on the table, which then starts
N|to shake. The two eggs and three small pieces of fruit then lift off the
N|table and float in the air. The children watch with awe.
O|
M|[This introduction of "magical" powers that E.T. possesses was unexpected
M|and thereby helps to make the story unpredictable.]
O|
N|Elliott screams with fright and the floating objects drop to the floor. He
N,U|walks to a lamp and stares into it. Mike asks what is wrong. "I don't know.
N,U,C|Something scary," says Elliott as E.T. places his finger on Elliott's
N|shoulder.
O|
M|[Jeopardy is introduced into the scene with this comment. Yet it is not
M|E.T. who is to be feared, as indicated by his comforting gesture of placing
M|his finger on Elliott's shoulder.]
O|
S|EXT: BACKYARD: NIGHT
O|
N|Elliott runs by the shed, past a swaying swing, up the stairs to the red
N|lamppost and to the wire gate. He strains to hear the sounds of men
N|talking.
O|
M|[The antagonist is getting closer to the home of the protagonist.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM: NIGHT
O|
N|E.T. sits next to a lamp as he reads a child's ABC book. He sits next to a
N|Raggedy Ann doll.]
O|
M|[Another image of E.T. learning the language which helps to forge a bond
M|between him and children in the audience. Good feelings for the doll are
M|also transferred to the alien.]
O|
N|On the floor next to E.T. is the pot containing the sickly flowers. He
N|looks at the flowers, makes a moaning sound, and suddenly they become
N|healthy and transform into full bloom.
O|
M|[This establishes the magical healing powers of E.T.]
O|
S|EXT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: DAY
O|
N|Elliott and Mike walk down the driveway. They are on their way to school.
N|They discuss E.T., arguing about how smart he is.
O|
M|[This is just a transition scene.]
O|
S|EXT: STREET: DAY
O|
N|Mike and Elliot walk towards a bus stop where a group of children are
N|waiting. Mike's friends torment Elliott about his "goblin."
O|
M|[This generates more audience empathy for Elliott, who is unfairly mocked
M|by the ignorant boys. Elliott still has not earned the respect of Mike's
M|friends, which is one of his dreams.]
O|
N|They finally get on the bus as Elliott walks away, followed by a pretty
N|blonde girl who seems to have a crush on him. Michael appears worried as he
N|sits on the bus among the other children who are throw- ing objects at each
N|other.
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: DAY
O|
N|Gertie stands on the stairway looking up, presumably thinking about E.T. in
N|Elliott's room. Mary comes running downstairs and tells Gertie that they
N|have to get into the car, otherwise they'll be late. Suddenly, Mary hears a
N|noise upstairs. She goes up to see what caused the sound.
O|
M|[Jeopardy: E.T. is in danger of being discovered by an adult. The
M|audience's expectations were shaped in the earlier dinner scene, where Mary
M|told the children she would call to have the creature taken away if he came
M|around the house again. This is what the audience expects her to do if she
M|finds E.T.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S BEDROOM: DAY
O|
N,G|Mary walks into the closet and looks around. The camera pans across the
N,G|large faces of all the stuffed dolls: Raggedy Ann, a Bear, a Monkey, E.T.'s
N|Face, a Lion, etc. Mary doesn't notice E.T., then closes the closet door
N|and leaves.
O|
M|[Humor is generated because of the incongruity of a living creature among
M|the dolls, and the inability of Mary to distinguish between them. The
M|laughter releases the tension that was created in the previous scene. This
M|also reinforces the audience's emotional ties to E.T., since he is so
M|"doll-like."]
O|
S|INT: SCHOOL: DAY
O|
N|A teacher walks down the aisle and announces to the class that today they
N|will perform the frog dissection for which they have been preparing. As he
N,U|passes Elliott's desk, he starts to say "frogs similar to," then he notices
N|the drawing of E.T. The teacher picks it up. Elliott turns to see the
N|pretty blonde girl staring at him. Realizing she's been caught, she quickly
N|turns and looks away.
O|
M|[This attraction to Elliott is set up in order to payoff before the end of
M|the school sequence.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM: DAY
O|
N|E.T. opens the door to Elliott's room and walks out into the hallway.
N|Harvey sees and quickly approaches.
O|
M|[Jeopardy: will the dog attack E.T.? At there last encounter E.T. was
M|frightened by Harvey.]
O|
T|INTERCUT: CLASSROOM
O|
N|The teacher continues his instructions, telling the students that the
N|scalpel is very sharp, and that they should use discretion when cutting.
N|There should be very little blood, and maybe a little body fluids.
O|
M|[This exposition of the plans sets up the audience's expectations, which
M|later will not be fulfilled. This is a standard structure used to generated
M|excitement and unpredictability in a story.]
O|
S|INT: KITCHEN: DAY
O|
N|E.T., wearing a blue-flannel shirt, walks into the kitchen with Harvey at
N|his side. He opens the refrigerator. Harvey licks his lips as he watches in
N|anticipation of getting something to eat. E.T. takes out a plastic
N|container of potato salad, opens it, then sticks his tongue into the salad.
N|He hates the taste and throws it onto the floor. Harvey licks it up. E.T.
N|then picks up a can of beer and drinks it as Harvey barks.
O|
T|INTERCUT: SCHOOL
O|
N|Elliott sits at his desk as he listens to the teacher speak. Elliott burps.
N|All the children in the classroom stare at him.
O|
M|[This again shows the merging of Elliott and E.T.]
O|
T|INTERCUT: KITCHEN
O|
N|E.T., drunk, wobbles across the kitchen floor and walks into a counter.
O|
T|INTERCUT: SCHOOL
O|
N|Elliott places his fingers to his head. He is also becoming drunk. The
N|teacher tells the class that in this bisection they should locate the heart
N|and notice that it is still beating.
O|
M|[The red-lit heart is the most prominent feature of E.T. Having the teacher
M|focus on the frog's heart starts the identification in the audience's mind
M|of the frogs and E.T.]
O|
T|INTERCUT: KITCHEN
O|
N|E.T. turns and walks into another wall as Harvey continues to eat the
N|potato salad on the floor.
O|
T|INTERCUT: SCHOOL
O|
N|Elliott, now drunk, slides down in his chair.
O|
T|INTERCUT: KITCHEN
O|
N|E.T. turns, walks, then collapses head first onto the floor.
O|
T|INTERCUT: SCHOOL
O|
N|Elliott slides under his desk and falls onto the floor. The teacher doesn't
N|hear this because at that moment he pulls down a wall hanging which
N|contains pictures of a frog's anatomy.
O|
T|INTERCUT: KITCHEN
O|
N|E.T. opens another can of beer.
O|
T|INTERCUT: SCHOOL
O|
N|Elliott pulls himself up off the floor with a drunken smile on his face.
O|
T|INTERCUT: KITCHEN
O|
N|E.T. gulps down another can of beer.
O|
T|INTERCUT: SCHOOL
O|
N|Elliott, drunk, sits at his desk. He turns and smiles at the pretty blonde
N|girl across the aisle. She turns away from him with disgust.
O|
T|INTERCUT: LIVING ROOM
O|
N|E.T. plays on the keys of a voice generating language game. He next presses
N|a button on a remote control which activates the TV set. A cartoon appears
N|with a cat getting its tail caught on fire. The cat screams and E.T reacts
N|with horror. He throws the beer can at the TV set.
O|
M|[Laughter is generated, for while this under normal circumstances would be
M|inappropriate behavior, he makes sense given E.T.'s point of view.]
O|
N|He then presses another button on the remote, and the image of a flying
N|plane appears on the screen. A spaceship next appears, and pulls the plane
N|up towards it.
O|
T|INTERCUT: SCHOOL
O|
N|The teacher carries a jar of cotton balls soaked in chloroform, which he
N|drops into the jars containing frogs. He tells the children to immediately
N|put the lids on the jars. After telling the children that this won't hurt
N|the frogs, he places a cotton ball into the last two jars, one of which
N|belongs to Elliott. Elliott doesn't place the cover on his jar, but instead
N|sadly watches as the frog tries to escape. Slowly he places to lid on it,
N|as he gazes at the frog.
O|
N,U,C|"Say hi. Can you talk? Can you talk? Can you say hi?" says Elliott.
O|
M|[Elliott is making an emotional identification of the frog with E.T. by
M|attempting the same process of communication. The situation also creates
M|expectations in the audience that similar things will happen to E.T. once
M|he is captured.]
O|
N,G|CLOSEUP: FROG'S FACE
O|
N,G|CLOSEUP: E.T.'S FACE
O|
M|[A cinematic technique which reinforces the identification of the frog with
M|E.T. This sets up the audience for E.T.'s operation. They expect the frog
M|to die, and they project these expectations to E.T. later in the story.]
O|
T|INTERCUT: LIVING ROOM
O|
N|E.T. has a hangover as he hiccups and puts his hand on his head. He then
N|picks up the cartoon section of a newspaper. One cartoon strip has a
N|picture of a communication device with a caption stating "HELP, HELP."
O|
N|On the TV monitor a woman is talking long distance n her telephone to her
N|Uncle Ralph in California. E.T. hears the sounds coming from the TV, then
N|turns to look at the monitor, where he sees a man talking into a phone in
N,G|his hand. The camera pans to a phone near E.T. A child in the TV program
N|picks up the phone, says hello to his uncle, then hangs up.
O|
M|[This generates laughter because it's inappropriate behavior to just say
M|hello, then hang up.]
O|
N|E.T. looks closer at the Buck Rogers cartoon. One cell has an image of a
N|man in a spacesuit setting up a transmitter. The next cell has him saying
N|"It works" as the communication device transmits "help, help!" E.T. lifts
N|up his head from the newspaper.
O|
T|INTERCUT: SCHOOL
O|
N,U,P,C|"Save him" shouts Elliott. Elliott picks up his jar, unscrews the top, then
N,P,C|lets out his frog, screaming "Run for your life. Back to the forest! Run!"
O|
M|[Another merging of E.T. and Elliott's feelings and thoughts. E.T.
M|presumably had the feeling of wanting to be saved by his friends, and this
M|feeling prompted Elliott to cry out.]
O|
N|He next opens the jar of the girl besides him. Elliott struggles with one
N|student, then proceeds to turn over many of the other jars as he frees the
N|frogs. Some of the other children also free their frogs.
O|
T|INTERCUT: LIVINGROOM
O|
N|E.T. studies the electronic circuitry of the language game. Suddenly John
N|Wayne comes on the screen. E.T. is fascinated with his image. It's a scene
N|from the movie "QUIET MAN." Maureen O'Sullivan huddles in a corner of the
N|room.
O|
T|INTERCUT: SCHOOL
O|
N|A pile of frogs gather at the feet of a terrified little girl with blonde
N|braids. Elliott, drunk, staggers by her.
O|
T|INTERCUT: LIVINGROOM
O|
N|E.T. continues to watch the TV monitor as the woman in the movie screams.
O|
T|INTERCUT: SCHOOL
O|
N|A blonde girl who has a crush on Elliott screams when a frog is placed on
N|her shoes.
O|
T|INTERCUT: LIVINGROOM
O|
N|The woman in the movie runs out the door. John Wayne grabs hold of her arm.
O|
T|INTERCUT: SCHOOL
O|
N|Elliott grabs hold of the blonde girl as she runs out the door.
O|
T|INTERCUT: LIVINGROOM
O|
N|John Wayne pulls the woman back.
O|
T|INTERCUT: SCHOOL
O|
N|Elliott pulls the blonde girl back.
O|
T|INTERCUT: LIVINGROOM
O|
N|John Wayne takes the woman into his arms.
O|
T|INTERCUT: SCHOOL
O|
N|Elliott takes the blonde girl into his arms.
O|
T|INTERCUT: LIVINGROOM
O|
N|John Wayne kisses the woman.
O|
T|INTERCUT: SCHOOL
O|
N|Elliott, too short to kiss her, stands on the back of a student who was
N|crawling on the floor after a frog. Elliott kisses the girl.
O|
T|INTERCUT: LIVINGROOM
O|
N|E.T., filled with fascination, watches the TV monitor.
O|
T|INTERCUT: SCHOOL
O|
N|The children's hands poke through the windows and free the frogs by
N|throwing them out onto the grass.
O|
M|[Elliott was able to accomplish his objective of freeing the frogs. This
M|sequence was structured to run in parallel in order to emphasize the
M|merging of Elliott and E.T.]
O|
S|INT: LIVING ROOM: DAY
O|
N|A blanket containing aluminum foil, electrical toys and appliances is
N|pulled across the floor.Mary opens the door and yells hello. Harvey runs to
N|the front door. E.T walks out from behind the TV and kicks beer cans across
N|the floor.
O|
M|[The alien quickly learns the appropriate behavior for drinking at home.]
O|
N|Mary, with her arms filled with grocery bags and clothes from the cleaners,
N|walks into the kitchen. She is followed by Gertie, who sees E.T standing
N|near the refrigerator. Mary opens the refrigerator door, and knocks E.T. in
N|the head. He falls backwards onto the floor. Gertie helps E.T. up off the
N|floor. He walks past Mary as he goes back to the refrigerator. Mary still
N|doesn't see him.
O|
M|[This increases the audience's tension. They are expecting E.T. to be
M|discovered by Mary, and they are expecting her to scream.]
O|
N|Gertie keeps telling her that she wants Mary to meet somebody, but Mary
N|ignores her as she talks about how much the price of food has gone up in
N|one week. E.T. grabs a can of coffee from the kitchen table, then again
N|walks past Mary as she goes in the opposite direction. He goes back into
N|the TV room.
O|
M|[The laughter's produced by Mary's failure to see the reality of the alien
M|who's right under her nose.]
O|
N|Gertie is watching a language teaching program for children on the TV. The
N|program is teaching words that begin with the letter "B". The phone rings
N|as Mary accidentally kicks a can of beer across the floor. She answers the
N|phone with the beer can in her hand. The person on the phone tells Mary
N|that Elliott is intoxicated.
O|
N|Gertie, watching the TV monitor, responds to the program by practicing the
N|"B" words. E.T. pokes his head up from behind the monitor and says
N,U|"BBBBBBB." Gertie stares at him as he repeats the "B" sound, then lowers
N|his head back down below the TV.
O|
N,U,C|"You said 'B'," says Gertie. "B," repeats E.T. "You said 'B,' good," says
N,U,C|Gertie. "B...GOOD," repeats E.T.
O|
M|[This is a set up for the closing communication between E.T. and Gertie
M|before he flies away in the spacecraft. This is also the first moment that
M|he spoke an English word. Many children and parents in the audience can
M|identify with this moment, and the first moral precept usually taught to a
M|child: be good.]
O|
N|Mary hangs up the phone and tells Gertie that she has to pick up Elliott.
N,U,C|She asks Gertie to be a good girl. "Mommy, he can talk," says Gertie. "Of
N,U,C|course he can talk," says Mary, referring to the TV program.
O|
M|[The humor is generated from Mary's misunderstanding of Gertie's
M|statement.]
O|
N|As Mary leaves, E.T. walks out from behind the TV. He walks to a phone and
N,U,C|stares. "Phone," says Mary. "Phone," says E.T. E.T. presses the numbers of
N,U,C|the phone. "You want to call somebody?" asks Gertie.
O|
M|[This establishes E.T.'s objective: to communicate with his fellow
M|creatures so that he can return home.]
O|
S|INT: HOUSE: DAY
O|
N|Elliott walks up the stairs to his room. Mary's voice can be heard from
N|below saying she refuses to pay for frogs.
O|
M|[This is a fine example of the point of attack for a scene: Instead of
M|showing what would happen in real life (Mary's discussions with the teacher
M|about Elliott's behavior in the classroom), the scene picks up with what is
M|important for the audience: Elliott's discovery that E.T. can now talk. A
M|scene showing Mary in school would be redundant and dramatically
M|irrelevant.]
O|
N|Elliott is walking towards his room when he hears Gertie talking to E.T. in
N|her room. He drops his bags on the floor, and quickly enters her room.
O|
N,U,C|"Be good, be good," says Gertie as she closes the doors to her closet.
N|Elliott pushes Gertie away from the closet and opens it. Inside is E.T.
N|dressed up in girl clothes with a blonde wig on his head. He has a rabbit
N|fur around his neck and wears a black hat with flowers.
O|
M|[This is completely inappropriate attire for an alien, whether male or
M|female. The visual incongruity of E.T. in girl's clothes generates the
M|laughter.]
O|
N,U,C|"Oh God!" says Elliott. "Elliott," says E.T. "What?" says Elliott, not yet
N,U,C,P|realizing that it was E.T. who spoke. "Elliott, Elliott," repeats E.T. "I
N,U,C|taught him how to talk. He can talk now," says Gertie.
O|
M|[Elliott, with the help of Gertie and the TV program, has completed one of
M|Elliott's objectives: communicating with E.T.]
O|
N|Gertie points out the things that E.T. has brought upstairs: electrical
N|toys and the plant. She wonders what he needs this for. E.T. holds the
N,U|cartoon page of the newspaper out to Elliott. "E.T., can you say that? Can
N,U,C,P|you say E.T.?" asks Elliott. "E.T.," repeats E.T. Elliott laughs with joy.
N|E.T keeps repeating his name as he turns and walks through the closet into
S|Elliott's room.
O|
N,U,C|"E.T., be good," says E.T. "Be good," I taught him that too," says Gertie.
O|
M|["Be good" is a fundamental value taught children, as basic as the
M|principle "There's no place like home," taught in the WIZARD OF OZ.]
O|
N,U|"You should give him his dignity. This is the most ridiculous thing I've
N,U,C|ever seen," says Elliott.
O|
N|E.T. hands Elliott the newspaper, and points to the Buck Rodgers comic
N,U|strip. He then says "PHONE". Elliott is amazed at this. E.T. walks into the
N,U|closet and says "HOME." Elliott confirms that this is E.T.'s home. E.T.
N,U|then turns, walks to the window, points to the sky, and says "E.T. HOME
N,U|PHONE."
O|
N,U,C|"E.T. PHONE HOME" says Gertie. "E.T. PHONE HOME" says Elliott.
O|
N,U,C|"He wants to call somebody," says Gertie.
O|
M|[This reconfirms E.T.'s objective.]
O|
N|Mike walks into the room and laughs at E.T.'s attire.
O|
N,U,C|"E.T. Phone Home," says E.T.
O|
N,U,C|"Oh my God, he's talking now," says Mike.
O|
N|E.T. once again points out the window and repeats that he wants to call
N|home.
N,U,C|"And they'll come?, says Elliott.
N,U,P|"Come?" responds E.T. "Home, home" he repeats.
O|
S|EXT: STREET: NIGHT
O|
N|A truck drives down the street. Inside a man with earphones listens to the
N|conversations in the neighborhood homes, as he fine tunes his electronic
N|eavesdropping equipment. He focuses in on Elliott's voice.
O|
M|[Jeopardy: the forces of the antagonist are getting closer and closer. They
M|are now inside the privacy of the protagonist's home, listening to their
M|most intimate conversations.]
O|
S|INT: GARAGE: NIGHT
O|
N|Elliott, Mike and Harvey are in the garage searching for objects that E.T.
N|could use in building his communications device. Elliott restates that he
N|has "absolute power" and that E.T. belongs to him.
O|
M|[Elliott's objective is to possess E.T., not set him free. Freeing the one
M|he loves is the lesson he will have to learn in this story.]
O|
S|EXT: STREET: NIGHT
O|
N|The van containing electronic eavesdropping equipment drives past Elliott's
N|house.
O|
M|[They are still in jeopardy: their actions and words are being monitored.]
O|
S|INT: GARAGE: DAY
O|
N|Mike tells Elliott that E.T. doesn't look too good anymore.
N,U,C|"Don't say that, we're fine!" says Elliott.
N,U|"What's all this we stuff. You say we all the time now. Look Elliott,
N,U,P,C|I think he might be getting sick," replies Michael.
O|
M|[This confirms Elliott's belief that he considers himself and E.T. to be
M|one being. It also introduces further jeopardy for E.T. Besides being
M|tracked by the antagonist, he is also becoming sick.]
O|
S|INT: VAN: NIGHT
O|
N|The man with the earphones is listening to Elliott and Michael's
N|conversation as they discuss E.T.'s sickness.
O|
S|INT: GARAGE: DAY
O|
N|Mike tells Elliott to grab the fuzzbuster. Elliott pulls his father's blue
N|shirt out from the shelf. They reminisce about the days when he used to
N|take them to baseball games and the movies, where they would have baseball
N|fights. Mike says that they'll do that again, but Elliott doesn't really
N|believe him. They each smell their father's shirt.
N,U,C|"Old Spice," says Mike.
N,U,C|"Sea Breeze," says Elliott.
O|
M|[Here a prop (their father's shirt) is used to reveal the emotional
M|relationships between the two boys and their father. Audience empathy is
M|generated for them because they have been abandoned by their father. The
M|audience's anger is transferred from the father to the antagonist who is
M|eavesdropping on this private intimate moment.]
O|
S|INT: VAN: NIGHT
O|
N|The man in the van continues to listen to Elliott and Michael's
N|conversation. Only the listener's eye is clearly visible.
O|
M|[This is a cinematic technique to signify the "ever-watching eye" of the
M|faceless antagonist.]
O|
S|INT: GERTIE'S ROOM: NIGHT
O|
N|Mary lies in bed next to Gertie as she reads her the Peter Pan story. Mary
N|reads that Peter has defeated the Indians, but Wendy and the boys have been
N|captured by the pirates. He vows to rescue her. E.T. watches them from the
N|closet.
O|
M|[Here the notion of "a rescue" is introduced, and visually associated with
M|E.T. as he watches them from the closet. This is an emotional set up for
M|the audience, so that they will later be supportive of Elliott and the
M|boy's efforts to rescue E.T.]
O|
N|Mary reads the section of the Peter Pan story where Tinker Bell becomes
N|poisoned. Elliott comes into the closet, stands next to E.T., then closes
N|the door behind them.
O|
N,U,P,C|"Tinkerbell swallows the draft as Peter's hand reaches for it," reads Mary.
N,U|"Why T, you have drunk my medicine."
O|
N|Elliott pulls E.T. away from the door, then shifts through a box of items
N|that he brought up from the garage. He cuts his finger on the blade of a
N,U|power saw, then says "OUCH," as he holds his bleeding finger up into the
N|air. E.T. points his own finger towards Elliott. E.T.'s finger glows like a
N|laser light as he says "ouch." He then presses this lit finger on Elliott's
N|cut and heals it.
O|
M|[This establishes the fundamental goodness and healing power of E.T.]
O|
N|E.T. opens the slit in the closet door and continues to watch Mary read to
N|Gertie the part where Tinker Bell is dying.
O|
N,U|"She says she thinks she can get well again if children believe in fairies.
N,U|Do you believe in fairies? Say quick that you believe."
N,U,C|"I DO! I DO! I DO!" says Gertie.
N,U|"If you believe, clap your hands."
N|Both Gertie and Mary clap their hands. Elliott places a blanket around
N|E.T.'s shoulders and a scarf around his neck, as they listen to the rest of
N|the story.
N,U,P,C|"Thank you, thank you. And now to rescue Wendy," reads Mary. Elliott places
N|his arm around E.T.
O|
M|[The Peter Pan story is used to set up the audience's emotional reactions
M|to a fairy tale concerning near death and rescue. This emotional empathy
M|will then be transferred to E.T., another "fairy-like creature" when he
M|becomes sick and is rescued. Just as the audience felt like clapping for
M|Tinker Bell, they will applaud the children's efforts to save E.T.]
O|
S|EXT: HOUSE: NIGHT
O|
N|Exhaust flows from the tailpipe of a van. This is the same type of
N|poisonous exhaust image that the audience saw when vans drove into the
N|forest at the beginning of the movie.
O|
M|[Jeopardy: the forces of the antagonist are still nearby.]
O|
S|INT: BEDROOM: NIGHT
O|
N|Elliott, dressed in white long-johns, sits on his bunkbed as he watches
N|E.T. Michael stands next to him. Elliott comments that E.T. is putting
N|together his instrument, while Mike fears that it might blow up the house.
N|He also comments on the irregularity of E.T.'s breathing.
O|
M|[More jeopardy, both in Mike's fears of his family and home being
M|destroyed, and in E.T.'s illness becoming progressively worse.]
O|
N|E.T places his communication device over the Buck Rodgers comic strip.
O|
M|[This movement establishes a mental association between the device and the
M|spaceman's communication efforts in the comic strip.]
O|
N|Petals drop off the flower in the pot.
O|
M|[This represents E.T.'s failing health.]
O|
N|E.T. lifts up his head (which indicates he's using his telekinetic powers),
N|then lifts part of his communication device into the air. He next lifts up
N|the umbrella and opens it. Both objects float in the air in front of him.
O|
M|[E.T's primary objective is to go home. To accomplish this he must create a
M|communication device. This is his sub-objective, which appears to be
M|completed. He next must activate this device from the landing site and call
M|home. The obstacles he has to overcome are Mary, the community, and the
M|distance to the forest. These obstacles will be confronted in the next
M|scenes.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S ROOM: DAY
O|
N|Preparing for Halloween, Elliott paints his lips with makeup. He talks to
N|Gertie about her going out that night as a ghost. They review the evening
N|plans. She will meet them at the lookout.
O|
M|[This is an example of incomplete exposition which engages the audience.
M|They only talk about part of the plan: the meeting at the lookout. They
M|don't discuss anymore details, such as deceiving Mary into thinking that
M|Gertie is with them instead of E.T. The partial information forces the
M|audience to stay involved with the action and to create their own
M|conjectures about what will happen.]
O|
S|INT: CLOSET: DAY
O|
N|Mike helps Elliot put on his knapsack. Elliott then places a sheet over
N|E.T.
O|
S|INT: STAIRCASE: DAY
O|
N|E.T., covered with a sheet and clown shoes, walks down the staircase. The
N|clown shoes cover his three-toed feet.
O|
S|INT: HOUSE: DAY
O|
N|Mary walks through the house dressed in a tight fitting leopard skin dress
N|with facial makeup that makes her look like a cat. Mike tells his mother to
N|wait and not peek, as he and Elliott lead E.T. into the room. Finally, Mary
N|turns and shrieks with glee. She runs to get a camera.
O|
M|[The tension in the situation is caused by their fear that E.T. will be
M|discovered by Mary. Mike and Elliott's objective is to get him out of the
M|house undiscovered.]
O|
N|E.T. looks through holes cut out for his eyes and sees Michael with a fake
N|butcher knife through his head. He lights up his finger in an attempt to
N|heal Mike's wound. Mike grab's E.T.'s hand and puts it back under the
N,U,C|sheet. "Ouch, ouch" says E.T, as he tries once again to heal Mike. "It's a
N,U|fake knife, it's fake," says Mike.
O|
M|[Laughter is generated because while E.T.'s behavior would be appropriate
M|if Mike was really wounded, it is inappropriate in this situation since the
M|knife is fake.]
O|
N|Mary comes back with a camera and prepares to photograph them.
O|
N,U,C|"You look great," says Mary.
N,U,C|"Thank you," says Mike.
N,U,C|"Thank you," says Elliott.
N,U,C|"Thank you," says E.T.
N|Mary doesn't notice that E.T. has spoken.
O|
M|[Laughter is generated by the "rule of three" humor structure and Mary's
M|ignorance.]
O|
N|When Mary takes the photo, the flash shocks E.T. and knocks him onto the
N|floor. Mike and Elliott quickly pull him up before Mary notices and lead
N|him out of the house.
O|
M|[They succeed in their objective with the help of their "not so smart"
M|mother.]
O|
S|EXT: STREET: DAY
O|
N|Mary walks down the driveway as she orders them to be home no later than
N|one-hour after sundown. E.T., dressed in the sheet, turns and looks at
N|Mary, then continues down the driveway with Mike and Elliott.
O|
M|[This creates a "timelock", a sense of urgency, and the expectation of an
M|upset mother if they haven't returned by the assigned time.]
O|
S|EXT: COMMUNITY: DAY
O|
N,G|Establishing shot of the neighborhood. On a hilltop by a fence stands
N|another person in a white sheet (presumably Gertie). She kicks dirt at the
N|bicycle near the fence.
O|
M|[This is the end objective of Mike, Elliot and E.T.: to connect up with
M|Gertie at the lookout. Their obstacle is to get E.T past all the children
M|on the streets.]
O|
S|EXT: STREET: DAY
O|
N|The sun is setting. Children in Halloween costumes walk through the
N|streets. E.T. walks between Mike and Elliott, as he watches the other
N|children parade by. One child dressed as YODA from RETURN OF THE JEDI walks
N,U|by. E.T. turns and walks after him saying "HOME,HOME."
O|
M|[The laughter is obviously the result of E.T. misinterpreting the situation
M|by believing that the child is another alien.]
O|
N|They walk towards the setting sun as Mike reminds Elliott that he must be
N|back by one hour after sunset. Elliott promises to try, but he wants Mike
N|to cover for him.
O|
M|[This creates jeopardy for the characters, because if they don't complete
M|their mission within the time frame, they will have trouble with Mary.]
O|
S|EXT: HILLTOP: DAY
O|
N|The sun is setting as Mike, Elliott, and two small characters covered with
N|sheets stand on the hilltop overlooking the city. Elliott asks Mike to help
N|him lift E.T. onto the bike. Mike does and then tells Elliott that they
N|will be waiting for him.
O|
M|[They have reached this objective. The next objective for Elliott and E.T
M|is to get to the landing site in the forest.]
O|
S|EXT: FOREST: DAY
O|
N|Elliott, with E.T sitting in the front basket, rides his bicycle through
N|the forest. The road becomes very bumpy. Elliott tells E.T. that they will
N|have to walk from there. Suddenly, E.T. takes control of the bike through
N|the use of his telekinetic powers and speeds it forward. Eliott becomes
N|frightened as E.T. speeds the bike towards a cliff and then drives it over
N|the edge. E.T. then makes the bike up through the air.
O|
M|[The audience gulps from suspense and then goes into elation by this
M|surprising and magical turn of events.]
O|
N,C,P,U|They continue to fly over the tree tops. Elliott yells "Not so high, not so
N,U|high."
O|
M|[E.T and Elliott both have white hoods over their heads, which further
M|visually reinforces their identification.]
O|
N|They ride across the front of the moon, the guiding light of magic. Elliott
N|laughs with glee. When they start their descent he begs E.T. not to crash.
N|They hit the ground. The bike turns over, and both E.T. and Elliott are
N|thrown to the ground.
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: NIGHT
O|
N|The grandfather clock indicates 9:45. Mary watches the clock as she sits in
N|front of lighted candles. The time has long passed when Elliott was
N|supposed to be home. The lighted candles give a church like effect as if
N|Mary was praying for the safety of her children. She is upset that they
N|haven't returned. She blows out the candles, then taps out another candle
N|with her wand.
O|
M|[There is jeopardy for the children as Mary and the audience wonder if they
M|are okay. Empathy is generated for Mary who is truly concerned for the
M|safety of her children.]
O|
S|EXT: FOREST: NIGHT
O|
N|The sun has set. It's nighttime in the forest. E.T opens a box which
N|contains the saw blade brought to him by Elliott. Elliott ties a rope up to
N|a tree branch. He then opens the umbrella which is lined with aluminum foil
N|and points it towards the star-filled sky. It will function as a
N|transmitter. E.T. places a coat hanger with nails above the saw blade,
N|plugs a cable into a battery, then activates the toy language teaching
N|device.
O|
M|[They succeeded in completing their objective to reach the landing site.
M|Their new objective is to communicate with E.T.'s fellow aliens in order to
M|accomplish E.T.'s primary objective, which is to return home.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: NIGHT
O|
N|Mary, still dressed in her leopard outfit, leaves the house. She is very
N|upset that her children have not yet returned home. She mumbles to herself,
N|sticks the wand in her mouth, and shakes the car keys. She gets into the
N|car and mumbles that their father is going to hear about this one.
N,U|"Mexico!" she says. She drives down the driveway and into the street to
N|search for her children.
O|
M|[This creates more empathy for Mary, who is very upset that her small
M|children are out this late. The parents in the audience can easily identify
M|with her. But the real function of this scene is to get her out of the
M|house so that the antagonist can invade their home. She could have stayed
M|inside, we never see her in the car searching for the children, and the
M|next time we do see her is by the refrigerator in the kitchen.]
O|
N|Men get out of a parked car as Mary drives away. They take suitcases out of
N|a second car, then walk up the driveway to Elliott's house.
O|
S|EXT: FOREST: NIGHT
O|
N|Elliott watches the treetops sway. He scratches his face, as does E.T.
N|Together they stare at the stars. The wind causes the branch to sway, which
N|then pulls the rope, which, in turn, pulls a fork across the saw blade.
N|This drags the nails in the hanger across the flat surface of the blade,
N|and sends signals out into space. Elliott is overjoyed and shouts that it
N,C,P,U|works, while E.T. repeats the word "Home." Together they gaze up to the
N|stars.
O|
M|[They have accomplished their objective of sending a message into space.
M|Now, they wait to receive a signal back.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: NIGHT
O|
N|The light from a flashlight crosses in front of a pumpkin's face. More
N|light floods through the slits of Gertie's door. A hand holding a
N|flashlight opens Gertie's door, and shines the light into the face of a
N|black doll.
O|
M|[This has an irritating and frightening effect on anyone in the audience
M|who has ever had the experience of having a light shined into their face.]
O|
N|Light from the flashlight searches around Gertie's room.
O|
S|INT: HALLWAY: NIGHT
O|
N|A pile of yellow electrical cord is stacked on the floor near a chair. It
N|slowly unwinds as it is pulled by someone in Elliott's room. The door to
N|Elliott's room is shut, with a poster sign hanging on it saying "DO NOT
N|ENTER." The silhouette of a man passes behind the door, as sounds of a
N|Geiger counter filter into the air. Muffled voices are heard as the cord is
N|pulled to its fullest extent. It forces a chair to slam against the wall.
O|
M|[This last action has a jarring effect on the audience. Emotionally, the
M|audience is outraged at the invasion of the children's rooms, and this
M|creates hatred for the antagonist and his cohorts. They are still faceless
M|so the audience can project the face of the persons they most hate onto
M|these characters. This personalizes the story for them and gets them more
M|intensely involved on an emotional basis. They also become intellectually
M|involved, because the sounds of the Geiger counter and the extension of the
M|cord to its maximum length forces them to infer that the antagonist has
M|found E.T.'s "home" in the closet. This involves the audience in the story
M|much more than if the filmmaker had shown the antagonist poking around in
M|the closet.]
O|
S|EXT: FOREST: NIGHT
O|
N,U,C|"We have to go now E.T. It's so late already," Elliott says as both he and
N|E.T. sadly gaze into the sky.
O|
M|[Their objective of receiving a return signal has not been achieved. Their
M|sadness at failure creates empathy for them in the audience.]
O|
N|Elliott coughs from being out in the cold, damp air.
O|
M|[This is a setup for the later scenes when both Elliott and E.T. are
M|deathly ill.]
O|
N|Elliott repeats that they really have to go home, but E.T. ignores him and
N|has a sad expression on his face as he stares up to the sky. Elliott tells
N|them that he has to give them some time. E.T. responds by saying "ouch,"
N|signifying that he feels pain. A small fawn watches them from a distance.
O|
M|[The jeopardy in the scene is due to their being so late. Mary will be very
M|angry and they face the obstacle of getting E.T. back into the house
M|without being discovered. The fawn generates feelings of sympathy in the
M|audience which is transferred to E.T. The audience also empathizes with the
M|sadness they feel at failing to make contact. For now, supposedly, E.T.
M|won't be able to go home.]
O|
N,U,C|"You could be happy here. I could take care of you," says Elliott
N,U|tearfully. I wouldn't let anybody hurt you. We could grow up together,
N,U|E.T."
O|
M|[Elliott states his primary objective: to always have E.T. with him.]
O|
N,U,C|"Home, Home," says E.T.
O|
M|[E.T. states his primary objective: to go home.]
O|
N|Elliott sits down and cries. E.T. touches one of his tears.
O|
M|[The audience empathizes with the sadness of both characters.]
O|
S|EXT: FOREST: NIGHT
O|
N|It's morning. Elliott is asleep as he leans against a large rock. He is
N|covered with a blanket as the wet forest mist surrounds him. Next to him
N|lies E.T.'s communication device.
O|
N|Elliott coughs, gets up, tugs on the rope which activates the device, then
N|looks around for E.T. but can't find him.
O|
M|[The new objective: where is E.T. and what has happened to him? This is
M|also the new question that will maintain the audience's interest.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: DAY
O|
N|Mary opens the refrigerator door and puts back a carton of milk. Mike and
N|Gertie sit besides her. A policeman asks Mary how Elliott was dressed when
N|she last saw him. Mary tells him that he was dressed as a hunchback. The
N|policeman, whose face is not shown (I suppose so that the audience will
N|associate him as "one of them"), asks Mary if there is any indication that
N|he might have run away? Were there any family problems or recent arguments?
N|Mary explains that she and her husband had just separated and that this has
N|not been easy on the kids.
O|
N,U,C|"My father is in Mexico," says Gertie.
O|
M|[This scene generates empathy for the whole family.]
O|
N|Mary slams the refrigerator door shut, thereby revealing Elliott standing
N|behind it. White light floods onto him making him look ghastly. Mary hugs
N|him as Mike and Gertie join them.
O|
M|[The audience is relieved and overjoyed with Elliott's safe return.]
O|
N,U,C,P|"Oh Elliott," she says as she hugs him. "Don't ever do this again, Elliott.
N,U|You're so hot."
O|
N|Elliott is feverish from spending the night outside. Mary tells Gertie to
N|go upstairs and draw him a bath. Mary walks over to the policeman (whose
N|face is still unseen) and thanks him for his concern.
O|
N|Elliott asks Mike if E.T. is in the house. Mike shakes his head, indicating
N|no. Elliot pleads with Mike to find him. He's somewhere in the forest near
N|the bald spot.
O|
M|[This is the new objective: find E.T. and bring him back.]
O|
S|EXT: STREET: DAY
O|
N|Mike rides the bicycle with the basket in front down the driveway and into
N|the street, past a car filled with waiting men. The car starts and follows
N|Mike. He looks behind and realizes that he is being pursued.
O|
M|[This is a standard chase scene, with a friend who is trying to save the
M|life of the protagonist being pursued. Both he and the protagonist are in
M|jeopardy. Mike's new sub-objective is to lose those who are following him.]
O|
N|Mike cuts through a backyard and into an alley behind some houses. The car
N|follows him. Mike then rides up an embankment and drives off in the
N|opposite direction, leaving the car stranded behind.
O|
M|[He successfully accomplished his subobjective.]
O|
S|EXT: FOREST: DAY
O|
N|Mike rides through the forest as he calls out E.T.'s name. He stops and
N|searches for E.T. Mike finds the abandoned communication device, then runs
N|up a path and discovers E.T.'s white sheet hanging from the open gate.
O|
M|[The blue-orange colors of E.T.'s communication device are the same colors
M|that Spielberg chose the logo of his production company, AMBLIN
M|ENTERTAINMENT.]
O|
M|[This is the same gate near the landing site that the characters ran past
M|in the opening sequence.]
O|
N|In another part of the forest, Mike rides his bike on a ridge above a
N|stream. E.T lies in the water below, is skin discolored to that of sickly
N|white. Mike sees him, grabs the sheet, then runs down the embankment. A
N|raccoon wanders near E.T. Mike shouts him away, then covers E.T. Mike hears
N|the sounds of a helicopter above him and looks up at the sky.
O|
M|[Mike has achieved his objective: he has found E.T. But, danger hovers
M|above him in the unseen presence of the ever observing antagonist.]
O|
S|EXT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: DAY
O|
N|The shadow of a man falls on the driveway. The sound of keys jangling is
N|heard.
O|
M|[High jeopardy: the antagonist is about to invade the home of the
M|protagonist.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: DAY
O|
N|Mike is in the hallway as Mary walks up the stairs. She has a cup of coffee
N|in her hand.
O|
M|[The cup is a prop which will be used to reveal Mary's emotions.]
O|
N|He wants her to come with him. He asks her if she remembers the goblin. He
N|tries to get her to make a promise, but she is annoyed with him.
O|
S|INT: BATHROOM: DAY
O|
N|Michael then opens the door to the bathroom. Elliott is sitting on the
N|floor as Gertie sits on the counter. They are both looking into the
N|bathtub. Mary smiles as she walks into the bathroom with the cup of coffee
N|in her hand. Then she sees E.T. on the floor. He is a sickly white. He
N|extends his arms out towards her. Mary is stunned, as she turns the cup
N|over and coffee pours out onto the floor.
O|
M|[Here a character's non-verbal action with a prop reveals her true
M|emotional state. She has lost it.]
O|
N,U,C|"He's sick. I think we're dying," says Elliott. Mary covers her mouth. Mike
N|tries to tell her that E.T. won't harm them, but she orders him to take
N|Gertie downstairs. Gertie cries that he's the man from the moon as Mike
N|picks her up and carries her downstairs.
O|
M|[Elliott, clothed only in white long-john underwear, is visually almost
M|identical to the ghastly white-colored E.T.]
O|
N|Mary picks up Elliott and carries him out of the room as E.T. groans and
N|reaches out for them.
O|
M|[Mary's objective is to save her family from the alien in her home.]
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: DAY
O|
N|Mary carries Elliott down the stairs. He protests that she can't leave E.T.
N|alone. Michael runs down the stairs with Gertie on his back. He opens the
N|door, freezes, then backs away. Mary, with Elliott in her arms, approaches
N|the door and is confronted by a man in a spacesuit.
O|
N|Mary backs into the living room as the spaceman enters and reaches out for
N|her. She goes in the opposite direction, only to run into another man in a
N|spacesuit. She runs into the kitchen, followed by Mike carrying Gertie. The
N|are blocked by still another spaceman with outstretched arms. Mary and her
N|children are backed into a corner. Another man in a spacesuit opens up the
N,C,P,U|window blinds from the outside. Mary shouts "THIS IS MY HOME."
O|
S|EXT: STREET: DAY
O|
N|Dawn. The sun rises as a line of men wearing helmets and uniforms march up
N|the street.
O|
S|INT: BATHROOM: DAY
O|
N|E.T., his skin discolored to a grey-white hue, lies on the floor as a man
N,U,C|in a space suit enters the room. "Home," says E.T.
O|
M|[This is the end of act two. The antagonist and his forces have invaded the
M|home of the protagonist and taken them into possession. This terrifying
M|invasion of the privacy of their home generates more empathy for Mary and
M|her children.]
O|
S|EXT: STREET: DAY
O|
N|Men in helmets walk up a street as they push a large plastic tunnel. Police
N|cars slowly drive up the street, as faceless men march to a drumbeat.
O|
M|[This is the beginning of the third act with the forces of the antagonist
M|in complete control.]
O|
S|EXT: STREET: NIGHT
O|
N|Men with welding torches work on a truck. The plastic tunnel leads up the
N|driveway to Elliott's house. The faces of the men are still not shown. They
N|are either wearing helmets or are silhouettes.
O|
N,G|CLOSEUP
O|
N|The man with keys jangling on his side is putting on a pair of light-blue
N|pants.
O|
T|INTERCUT: STREET
O|
N|Men are working outside. Large bright lights flood the area.
O|
T|INTERCUT: "KEYS"
O|
N|The man in the blue suit puts on a white pair of plastic gloves. Red and
N|black colors on his cuff are associated with his keys as ominous music
N|fills the soundtrack.
O|
M|[This is the only instance in this film where the traditional colors of the
M|antagonist (red and black) are associated with the antagonist ("keys").
M|Putting on a light-blue outfit starts the transformation of the antagonist
M|into a character worthy of audience empathy, for blue-white is the
M|traditional production design colors signifying "innocence."]
O|
N|The man in the light-blue suit gets up walks through the plastic tunnel
N|towards Elliott's house. He exits the tunnel and walks into the house which
N|has been completely enclosed by plastic.
O|
N|Finally, the antagonist's face is shown. His head is covered by a blue hood
N|while a light under his face mask accentuates his white shirt.
O|
N|Mary stands in the middle of the room with Gertie at her side. A doctor
N|asks her questions about E.T., but Mary is more concerned with Elliott's
N|state. A nurse cuts a lock from Gertie's hair (presumably for analysis),
N|while both Elliott and E.T. can be seen on a video monitor. This nurse
N|walks to Mike and cuts a piece of his hair while a doctor asks him
N,U|questions about E.T. A video monitor shows Elliott and E.T. "Elliott thinks
N,U,C|his thoughts," asks the doctor. "No, Elliott feels his feelings," answers
N,C|Michael.
O|
M|[This is the first time in the film that the merging of Elliott and E.T.
M|has been explicitly stated. Yet, this condition has been revealed to the
M|audience non-verbally throughout the story. The verbalization seems
M|redundant and not necessary, but does make sense as an explanatory comment
M|to a character new to the story.]
O|
S|INT: CHAMBER: DAY
O|
N|A man stands in what appears to be a decontamination chamber.
O|
S|INTERCUT: MEDICAL INSTRUMENTS
O|
N|Hands push buttons on electronic equipment used to gather medical
N|information about Elliott and E.T. Graphs representing E.T. and Elliott run
N|in exact parallel.
O|
M|[This is another representation of the fact that E.T. and Elliott have
M|merged and are to be considered as one being.]
O|
S|INT: MEDICAL ROOM: DAY
O|
N|Two people in white suits zip open a plastic covering and enter the room
N|where Elliot and E.T. are being examined.
O|
M|[The color white traditionally signifying purity. A transition in the
M|audience's emotional alliances is being manipulated at this stage of the
M|story. Their hatred will be transferred from "keys" who will become much
M|more sympathetic to "death itself" which threatens the life of E.T. It
M|would be too much to ask of the adult audience to remain sympathetic to the
M|emotional pleadings of a child when space scientists are trying to save the
M|life of a sick alien.]
O|
N,U,P,C|"You're scaring him," cries Elliott, as the doctors examine both Elliott
N|and E.T. as they lie on adjacent tables. E.T's head rests on a blue piece
N|of cloth.
O|
N,U,P,C|"Leave him alone, leave him alone. I can take care of him," moans Elliott.
O|
M|[Which, of course, he can't. But this is something that both he, and
M|probably most of the children in the audience, really believe.]
O|
N|Elliott looks up into the face of "Keys," who says that he's been to the
N|forest. He wants to know what the machine does. Elliott says that it's a
N|communication machine, and asks if it's still working. He then states that
N,U|"He came to me. He came to me." "Keys" explains that he's been wishing for
N|this too, ever since he was ten years old. He doesn't want the alien to
N|die. He asks what more can they do that they are not already doing.
O|
N|Elliott's image reflects in "Keys" face gear. Elliott tells him that E.T.
N|was calling his people because he needs to go home.
O|
M|["Keys" is no longer the threat. He has been transformed into a friend who
M|also wants to help the alien. A new antagonist must now be created in order
M|to maintain conflict in the story. In this scene DEATH will serve that
M|function. For the rest of the film the forces of the United States
M|Government, who attempt to block E.T.'s escape, will function as the
M|antagonist.]
O|
N|Another Doctor dressed in white enters the room and announces that E.T. has
N|DNA. Suddenly, a display screen shows a drop in E.T. and Elliott's graphs.
N|One doctor says there's a drop in the creature's blood temperature, while
N|another states that Elliott's condition is stabilizing: his blood pressure
N|is coming back up.
O|
N|E.T. calls Elliott's name, and the medical personnel become aware that he
N,C,P,U|can talk. Elliott pleads with him to stay. E.T. repeats the words "stay,
N,U|stay," then slowly turns his head away.
O|
N|Their graphs separate. One doctor announces that the boy is coming back,
N|but they are losing E.T.
O|
S|INT: CLOSET: NIGHT
O|
N|Michael enters the closet space where E.T. stayed. He looks at the objects,
N|including the cartoon page from the newspaper, then huddles down among
N|E.T.'s possessions.
O|
M|[These actions create more empathy for Michael who suffers at the thought
M|of losing E.T.]
O|
N|The is a time transition, for Michael is asleep in E.T.'s space when the
N|morning sun shines on his face. Michael wakes up, then watches the plant
N|(which represents E.T.'s life force) wilt. He screams.
O|
S|INT: MEDICAL ROOM: DAY
O|
N,U,P,C|"E.T., don't go" screams Elliott.
O|
N|Doctors state that he has no blood pressure and there is no pulse or
N|respiration. A bell rings, which signifies a life and death situation.
N|Doctors run into the room and tear down the plastic coverings surrounding
N|E.T.
O|
N,U,C|"Leave him alone. You're killing him," says Elliott. They wheel Elliott out
N|of the room as he continues to scream. The medical staff frantically tries
N|to revive E.T. They try electrical shocks, but that fails. Gertie holds her
N|doll as she watches. She reacts by jumping when the electrical shocks are
N|applied to E.T.
O|
M|[Showing one character's reactions to a situation help to create the
M|intended reaction in the audience.]
O|
N|The doctors are losing him. Elliott watches and cries. Mary goes to him and
N|they hug each other. Gertie cries as she holds her doll. Mike walks into
N|the room, as "Keys" watches, then turns away.
O|
M|[This provides the audience with each character's reaction to the death of
M|E.T. This is a surprising event for the audience: the protagonist has died.
M|Emotionally, it was designed to sadden the audience so that they will
M|become overjoyed when he is brought back to life.]
O|
S|EXT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: DAY
O|
N|A white van awaits at the bottom of the driveway.
O|
M|[Previously, the vans and cars associated with the antagonist were of dark
M|colors. The color changes allows the filmmaker to use culturally determined
M|color associations to manipulate the audience's emotional responses.]
O|
N|Police and other officials walk past a white van, white truck, police cars
N|and other white vehicles towards a tan colored car. Mike's friends sit on
N|their bikes behind police barriers. They make comments that something has
N|just happened inside the house.
O|
M|[This establishes the existence of Mike's friends who will play a crucial
M|role in the last chase scene.]
O|
S|INT: MEDICAL ROOM: DAY
O|
N|The medical staff gives up on E.T. and decide to pack him in ice. Mike
N|watches as the staff of E.T. slowly walks away. "Keys" removes his headgear
N|and walks to Elliott who is standing vigil over E.T.'s body. "Keys" then
N|approaches E.T. Mike watches and cries as a man behind him removes his
N|headgear. The faces of several medical attendants are shown.
O|
M|[This humanizes the forces of the antagonist and makes them much less
M|threatening. That's why they had to be faceless for so long throughout the
M|story.]
O|
N|"Keys" kneels besides E.T. and with gloved hand closes his eyes. Mary holds
N|Gertie. When Gerite asks if he's dead, Mary replies that she thinks so.
N|Gertie then says she wishes him back.
O|
M|[The interchange between Gertie and Mary reminds the audience of the Tinker
M|Bell situation when Mary read to her from Peter Pan. Those same feelings
M|are reintroduced here: the clapping of hands to save the fairy and then the
M|desire to rescue the children. This is the payoff to the emotional
M|reactions set up in that earlier scene.]
O|
N|"Keys" looks up to see two attendants roll in a metal casing filled with
N|ice into which they will place E.T.'s body.
O|
N|Mary picks up Gertie and carries her out of the room. Medical technicians
N|prepare the casing as Elliott watches. Keys approaches Elliot and tells him
N,U|that they will be taking E.T. away. "They are just going to cut him all
N,U,P,C|up," replies Elliott.
O|
M|[This is the payoff to the frog sequence. The audience believes that
M|Elliott is correct, given the scientific mind as demonstrated by the
M|teacher. They also now remember the joy they felt when Elliott freed the
M|frogs, and wish they could experience that again now.]
O|
N,U,C|"Do you want to spend some time alone with him?" asks "Keys," as he puts
N|his arm around Elliott and walks him to the casing. He tells the medical
N|attendants to leave the room.
O|
M|[This generates empathy for "Keys" who is shown to be concerned for
M|Elliott's feelings.]
O|
N|The attendants walk out of the room past Michael who is watching Elliott.
N|"Keys" leaves Elliott alone with the body of E.T., which is covered in a
N|plastic bag as it lies on dry ice. Elliott stares down at E.T.
O|
N,U|"Look at what they've done to you. I'm so sorry. You must be dead. I... I
N,U|didn't know how to feel. I can't feel anything anymore. You've gone
N,U|someplace else now. E.T., I love you."
O|
M|[Elliott wears a partially open white-jacket. He is framed within an oval
M|shaped glass.]
O|
N|Elliott places the cover down over E.T. and walks away from the oval glass.
N|A red-light flashes from inside the casing, but Elliott doesn't notice this
N|as he walks away. He passes the pot of wilted flowers. The flowers slowly
N|start to regain strength. Elliott notices this, then quickly returns the
N|casing.
O|
M|[This is the payoff for the pot-of-wilted-flowers prop.]
O|
N|He opens the casing, sees the red-light flashing from E.T.'s heart, then
N,C,U|zips open the plastic bag. The alien looks up and says "E.T. phone home."
N|Elliott screams with joy.
N,U,C|"Home, home, home, home," says E.T.
N,U,C|"Does this mean they are coming?" asks Elliott.
N,U,C|"Yes," answers E.T.
O|
M|[E.T. has finally succeeded in his objective of communicating home.]
O|
N|Elliott tells him to stay and shut up, as he places his hand over E.T.'s
N|mouth. Elliott zips him back up, then places a cloth over his flashing
N|red-heart, as "KEYS" re-enters the room. Elliott slams down the cover.
N|"KEYS" pulls Elliott away from the case as two attendants prepare for its
N|removal. Elliott cries out with feigned sorrow to conceal his glee when he
N|sees the wilted flowers regain their health. The attendants lock the case.
O|
S|INT: DECONTAMINATION CHAMBER: DAY
O|
N|Elliott tells Mike that E.T. is alive. Mike's also overjoyed. Mike and
N|Elliott rejoice as the wilted flowers continue to bloom.
O|
S|INT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: DAY
O|
N|Mary is talking to "KEYS" as Gertie runs up with the pot of flowers in her
N|hands. He asks Mary if the boys have gone because she's sup- posed to give
N|her a note when they have gone. Mary takes the note from Gertie and reads
N|it.
O|
M|[This note provides the crisis to which Mary must react, yet the audience
M|is kept in suspense.]
O|
S|EXT: PLASTIC TUNNEL: DAY
O|
N|Elliott runs down the tunnel past two men dressed in blue, then enters the
N|back of the van. Elliott then pulls aside the white curtains that separate
N|the drivers compartment from the back of the van to reveal Michael in the
N|driver's seat. He to is wearing a blue jacket. Elliott yells at Mike for
N|not wearing his mask. Suddenly, a black official walks by the van, spots
N|Mike in the driver's seat, and knocks on the window. He holds a
N|walkie-talkie in his hand as he asks Mike who he is. Mike responds that
N|he's driving. The official tells Mike to open the door. When Mike looks to
N|Elliott for advice, Elliott tells him to drive away.
O|
M|[Elliott and Mike's objective is to escape with E.T.]
O|
N|A group of men approach the van. Mike screams that he's never driven
N|forward before, shifts into first gear, and drives away.
O|
M|[This is the payoff to the scene of Mike incompetently backing the car down
M|the driveway.]
O|
N|As Mike drives away he drags along the plastic tunnel with the two men
N|still inside. Mike briefly stops by his friends and tells them to meet him
N|at the playground at the top of he hill. He then drives away, pursued by a
N|group of men. Mike's friends put on their hats and sun- glasses, and join
N|the fray.
O|
M|[The chase is on. Suspense is created because the audience wonders whether
M|they will get E.T. back to the forest before the are stopped. They face
M|impossible odds, yet Mike's friends display loyalty as they join the fight.
M|Mike and Elliott's first objective is to reach the park. Their major
M|obstacle is the men trying to capture them.]
O|
N|Mike drives the van down the street. The two men still in the plastic
N|tunnel try to climb back towards the van. Elliott starts to disconnect the
N|tunnel by pulling out metal braces.
O|
M|[Jeopardy: If these men get into the van, they will overpower Elliott and
M|Mike, and prevent E.T. from getting home.]
O|
S|EXT: ELLIOTT'S HOUSE: DAY
O|
N|Mary backs her car out of the garage. Gertie is sitting next to her in the
N|front seat holding onto the flower pot. "Keys" runs up to Mary and asks
N,C,P,U|where she's going. Gertie blunts out "to the spaceship."
O|
M|["Keys" now realizes that the aliens are returning for E.T. This is his
M|"crisis" information, that spurns him into action.]
O|
S|EXT: STREET: DAY
O|
N|The van continues down the road, still pulling the plastic tunnel behind
N|it. The two men have almost made it to the back of the van as Elliott pulls
N|out the final stake. He tosses it to one of the men. When Mike turns a
N|sharp corner the plastic tunnel disconnects, leaving the two men behind.
O|
M|[They have overcome their immediate obstacle. Humor is generated when
M|Elliott tosses the nail to one of the men, because it is the reversal of a
M|life saving situation. Instead of catching something (like a rope) that
M|will save him, he catches the object that is his undoing.]
O|
S|EXT: STREET: DAY
O|
N|A convoy of police cars drive up a street.
O|
M|[The chase is still on. Even though one obstacle has been overcome, the
M|antagonist brings more formidable forces into the fight. The protagonist is
M|still in jeopardy.]
O|
S|EXT: PARK: DAY
O|
N|The white van drives up over the sidewalk and into the park. It swerves
N|between the children's rides, then finally stops. Smoke pours out of the
N|van, whose back two doors are still open.
O|
M|[They have reached their sub-objective: the park where they are to meet up
M|with Mike's friends and continue on their bikes.]
O|
N|Mike's friends wait on their bikes. Between them they also have bikes for
N|Elliott and Mike. E.T. is revealed to the boys. He stands among the smoke
N,U|from the dry-ice with his red-heart light flashing. "Okay, he's a man from
N,U,C|outer space and we're taking him to his spaceship," says Elliott, now
N|dressed in blue pants, white shirt, and red jacket.
N,U,C|"Why can't he just beam up?" asks Greg.
N,U,C|"This is reality, Greg," says Elliott.
O|
M|[Of course, the joke is that it isn't reality. It is a fantasy movie with a
M|realistic response to a fantastic situation. Right?]
O|
M|[Elliott finally wins the respect and admiration of Mike's friends,
M|something he's desired from his introduction scene when they wouldn't allow
M|him to play DUNGEONS & DRAGONS with them.]
O|
S|EXT: PARK: DAY
O|
N|Mary, along with a crowd of plain clothes police, run towards the van.
N,U,P,C|"Don't shoot, they're only children," screams Mary.
O|
M|[This introduces a life and death jeopardy into the scene. It also
M|characterizes the forces of the antagonist as characters who would shoot
M|children.]
O|
N|They reach the back of the van only to find it empty.
O|
S|EXT: STREET: DAY
O|
N|Elliott and friends ride down the street. E.T., covered with a white
N|blanket, rides in Elliott's basket. Elliott, with the red hood over his
N|head, shouts that they are to ride to the forest.
O|
M|[To cloth a character in red is effective in chase scenes. This helps the
M|audience to visually pick out the scenes important character when the pace
M|is fast and the action intense. Spielberg used this technique for Elliot in
M|this scene and for Marion in RAIDERS OF THE LOST ARK when she runs through
M|the market chased by Arabs.]
O|
N|Cars chase them. Elliott rides up the alley and tells the others to follow
N|him. Police cars encircle them and try to cut them off. The bikers turn off
N|the main road and ride into a dirt field. A tan car with "UNITED STATES
N|GOVERNMENT" written on the driver's door rides parallel to the bikers.
O|
N|Two men ride inside the tan car. One speaks into the car radio saying that
N|they'll get them at the bottom of the hill. The tan car pulls into the base
N|of the hill and stops. One man gets out and starts to climb up the
N|embankment. The bikers almost run him over. They ride over the hood of the
N|car, then continue down the street, now pursued by a blue police car.
O|
N|The bikers cycle down a dirt road of a section of the community that is
N|still under development. E.T., with the white blanket covering his head,
N|sits in the basket of Elliott's bike. A blue car trails them. The bikers
N|decide to split up. Two ride down an embankment. They are followed by
N|another police car. The bikers then escape from their pursuers by riding
N|down steep embankments. The cars cannot follow and must stop on different
N|levels. The bikers unite and ride away.
O|
M|[It seems that the chase is over and they have eluded the antagonist.]
O|
N|The biker's are overjoyed by their victory. One takes off his hat, waves it
N,P,U|in the air, and shouts "We've made it!" Suddenly, he quickly ducks to avoid
N|being grabbed by a man. A group of men continue the chase. Several cars
N|pull up ahead and form a blockade in the road.
O|
N|Elliott and E.T. ride forward. Horror grows on Elliott's face as he stares
N|at the enemy in front of him.
O|
M|[This final confrontation between the protagonist (Elliott and E.T.) and
M|the antagonist.]
O|
N|The antagonist is explicitly revealed at this moment. On the side panel of
N|a car is a sign stating:
O|
N|                           FOR OFFICIAL USE ONLY
N|                              U.S. GOVERNMENT
N|                           INTERAGENCY MOTOR POOL
N|                                    GSA
O|
N|The shadow of a shotgun covers the sign. Next, two shotguns are raised
N|against E.T. and Elliott.
O|
N,G|Elliott shuts his eyes with fear. There is a closeup of E.T.'s face. With
N|shotguns drawn, government agents gesture for them to stop.
O|
M|[This is the life and death final moment before the climax. The audience
M|has been set up to expect that the government agents would shoot at the
M|protagonist by Mary's comments in the park scene.]
O|
N|Suddenly, E.T. saves the day with his telekinetic powers. He lifts all of
N|the bikers up into the air as they fly above the blockade.
O|
M|[The climax is over E.T. has saved himself and his friends]
O|
N|E.T. then flies the bikers over the housetops, and across the setting sun.
N|Finally they float down onto a path in the forest.
O|
S|EXT: FOREST: DAY
O|
N|Elliott has reached the landing site. He brushes the leaves away from the
N|turntable of the communication device. A blue-light floods Elliott's face.
N|He looks up into the, along with the other bikers. E.T., with red-heart
N|happily flashing, also gazes into the sky as his spaceship descends.
N,U,C|"Home," says E.T.
N|The spaceship continues its descent as E.T.'s red-heart flutters. The
N|spaceship finally lands in a golden glowing light.
O|
N|A car pulls into the forest, and Gertie's voice can be heard pointing out
N|the location of the boys. The ramp to the spaceship lowers. Gertie runs up
N|to Mike and E.T. She gives E.T. the plant and says that she just wanted to
N|say goodbye. Michael responds that E.T. doesn't know goodbye.
N,U,C|"Be good," says E.T.
N,U,C,P|"Yes," says Gertie, as she kisses him on the nose.
N|Mike places his hand on E.T.'s head.
O|
M|[This is a basic gesture of trust. The fact that E.T. allows Mike to do
M|this indicates that he considers him a friend.]
O|
N|E.T. puts the plant on the ground, as Mary and "Keys" run up to the edge of
N|the site. Mike picks up Gertie and carries her away as Elliott approaches
N|E.T.
O|
N,U,C|"Come," says E.T
N,U,C|"Stay," says Elliott.
O|
M|[The two characters that had merged into one protagonist must split apart
M|because they now have different primary objectives. E.T. want's to return
M|home, and Elliott wants him to remain.]
O|
N,U|E.T. lifts his finger to his lips and says "ouch."
O|
M|[Of course, this indicates the pain he feels in their separation.]
O|
M|Elliott tearfully lifts his finger to his lips and says "ouch."
O|
M|[The symmetry of his movements indicate that in some sense, they still are
M|one.]
O|
N|E.T. and Elliott hug each other as the are visually surrounded by blue and
N|white lights. Then Elliott and Mary exchange glances as Mike and Gertie
N|stand by and watch. E.T. lifts his finger to Elliott's forehead. It lights
N,U|up, then he says: "I'll be right here."
O|
M|[The resolution of the dilemma. Though E.T. leaves, he will still remain,
M|perhaps telepathically, or perhaps only within Elliott's thoughts.]
O|
N,U,C|"Bye," says Elliott..
O|
N|E.T. picks up the potted plant and carries it up the gangplank. Elliott
N|watches from below as Harvey starts up the plank, then returns to Elliott's
N|side.
O|
M|[E.T. leaves with what he really came for, a sample of the earth's
M|vegetative life form. But now he has much more in the flowers that will
M|serve as a memorial of all that he has experienced with his new friends.]
O|
N|The gangplank lifts up and separates E.T. from Elliott. The ships circular
N|entrance closes. The spaceship lifts off. Elliott watches it float up into
N|the sky. It then speeds away, leaving an orange-blue rainbow behind against
N|the blue-morning sky.
O|
M|[Orange and blue were also the colors of E.T.'s makeshift communication
M|device as found by Mike in the forest.]
O|
N|Mary watches and laughs with happiness. The other boys also watch, as Mike
N,G|holds Gertie in his arms. The film ends with a shot of Elliott gazing up
N|into the sky.
O|
M|THE END
O|
